<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Velletri</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Velitrae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Velletri is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. As Velitrae it was a Volscian town that Rome colonised several times in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE; a bronze tablet found there is the main surviving text in the Volscian language. It was the home of the family of Gaius Octavius, father of the emperor Augustus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/velletri.jpg" alt="Velletri" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deblu68 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Velletri.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Velletri lies about 40 km to the south-east of the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>. Its northern territory stands on the southern foothills of the Colli Albani range, formed about 150,000 years ago after the collapse of the volcano Laziale, while its southern boundary reaches the Pontine Marshes, whose reclamation began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> and was completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>. The square of the Trivium in the old town stands at 332 metres above sea level, and the climate is mild and very rainy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> term for a swamp was Velia, and the name Velitrae is thought to come from the same root. Tradition says the town came into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under Ancus Marcius, the fourth king of Rome, who besieged it after the Volsci plundered Roman territory; its elders surrendered and a treaty of peace and friendship followed. Its massive Volscian walls were razed in 338 BCE as a punishment after the final Roman conquest, and they were not rebuilt until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p>Velletri declined after it was sacked by Alaric the Goth in 410 CE. It was a bishopric, and after the Byzantine reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> it became an imperial city. In 592 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> joined the Diocese of Tres Tabernae to that of Velletri. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, and its motto, Est mihi libertas papalis et imperialis, claims liberty from both pope and empire. The bell tower of Santa Maria in Trivio was raised in 1353 in thanks for deliverance from the plague of 1348.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> (1740-1748), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> troops defeated the Austrian Habsburgs at the Battle of Velletri. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the town rebelled and was proclaimed a republic, which lasted until 1814. In 1849 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> defeated a Bourbon Neapolitan force there, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> was overwhelmed soon after. Fierce fighting took place around the town in 1944, after the Anglo-American landing at Anzio.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Velletri is an Italian comune in the Metropolitan City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Its Cathedral of St Clement was built in the 4th century over a pagan temple and rebuilt in 1659-1662. The Oreste Nardini Civic Archaeological Museum holds the Sarcophagus of the Labors of Hercules, of the 2nd century. The bronze sheets of Velletri, found in 1784 within the Church of St Francis, are kept at the National Archaeological Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. The surrounding country is known for its wine, and the town was a regular stop between Rome and Naples on the Grand Tour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Velletri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
